%>


<p class="govuk-body">
The API has application-restricted endpoints and user-restricted endpoints. How you authenticate depends on which type you’re calling.
</p>

<%= govuk_table(html_attributes: { class: "govuk-!-font-size-16" }) do |table|
  table.with_head do |head|
    head.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "Endpoint type")
      row.with_cell(text: "Authentication")
      row.with_cell(text: "Credentials")
      row.with_cell(text: "Purpose")
    end
  end
  table.with_body do |body|
    body.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "Application-restricted")
      row.with_cell(text: "HTTP Basic")
      row.with_cell(text: "Client ID and client secret")
      row.with_cell(text: "Access endpoints that do not need authorisation from a user or give access to user-restricted data.")
    end
    body.with_row do |row|
      row.with_cell(text: "User-restricted")
      row.with_cell(text: "OAuth 2.0 Bearer")
      row.with_cell(text: "Access token")
      row.with_cell(text: "Access protected, organisation-specific data with authorisation from a user at that organisation.")
    end
  end
end %>

<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Application-restricted endpoints</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
Application-restricted endpoints authenticate the calling application without needing authorisation from an end user. They do not give access to user-restricted data.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
To call these endpoints, use <%= govuk_link_to("HTTP Basic Authentication", "https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc7617/") %> with your client ID and client secret.
Join them with a colon (<code>client_id:client_secret</code>), Base64-encode the result, and send it in an <code>Authorization</code> header after the word <code>Basic</code>.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
For example, if your client ID is <code>client123</code> and your secret is <code>supersecretvalue</code>, your header would be:

<pre>
<code>
Authorization: Basic Y2xpZW50MTIzOnN1cGVyc2VjcmV0dmFsdWU=
</code>
</pre>
</p>

<%= govuk_warning_text(text: "Do not include your client ID or client secret in the body of the request.") %>


<p class="govuk-body">
You can check you’re calling the API correctly with your client credentials by using our test <%= govuk_link_to("Hello API", api_hello_documentation_path, no_visited_state: true) %>.
</p>

<h4 class="govuk-heading-s">Example request</h4>
<pre>
<code>
curl -X GET <%= @api_base_url %>/hello/client \
-H "Accept: application/json" \
-H "Authorization: Basic Y2xpZW50MTIzOnN1cGVyc2VjcmV0dmFsdWU="
</code>
</pre>


<h3 class="govuk-heading-m">User-restricted endpoints</h3>
<p class="govuk-body">
User-restricted endpoints give access to personal data. To use them, your application must get permission from the end user.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
A user from each organisation you work with must authorise your application before we can give you an access token for that organisation. 
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
To find out how to get an access token, start with <%= govuk_link_to("How authorisation works", api_documentation_authorisation_page_path("how-authorisation-works"), no_visited_state: true) %>. 
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
Once you have an access token, add it to an <code>Authorization</code> header in your request as an OAuth 2.0 bearer token.
</p>

<p class="govuk-body">
You can check you’re calling the API correctly with your access token by using our <%= govuk_link_to("Hello API", api_hello_documentation_path, no_visited_state: true) %>.
</p>

<h4 class="govuk-heading-s">Example request</h4>

<pre>
<code>
curl -X GET <%= @api_base_url %>/hello/user \
-H "Accept: application/json" \
-H "Authorization: Bearer ZFfQMVBFJxyBdBhMqGzTdqMQPU23mVVu"
</code>
</pre>
